1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,958
A contributor's third pull request to an
open-source evaluation library shows eleven

2
00:00:09,998 --> 00:00:14,081
commits. She wrote one. The
maintainer asks her to clean it up.

3
00:00:14,121 --> 00:00:19,318
She opens her fork, her own copy of the
repository on GitHub, and finds that its

4
00:00:19,358 --> 00:00:24,816
main is three commits ahead of upstream,
the original project, and forty-one behind.

5
00:00:24,856 --> 00:00:29,986
She never meant to commit on main. The
page offers to sync, and a search result

6
00:00:30,026 --> 00:00:31,950
suggests gh repo sync --force.

7
00:00:32,290 --> 00:00:36,968
A colleague who has done this before stops
her: what happens to the three commits if

8
00:00:37,008 --> 00:00:37,642
you do that?

9
00:00:37,982 --> 00:00:43,308
They are discarded. This video walks the
fork workflow from end to end, so that the

10
00:00:43,348 --> 00:00:48,739
fork's main never gets into that state, and
shows how to keep the stray work when it

11
00:00:48,779 --> 00:00:53,459
does. Keep those three commits in mind.
Before the end, we get them back.

12
00:00:55,299 --> 00:00:59,005
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:59,045 --> 00:01:04,162
You already have every piece of this. In
the remotes videos you built the triangular

14
00:01:04,202 --> 00:01:08,276
configuration: origin is your fork,
upstream the shared repository.

15
00:01:08,316 --> 00:01:11,469
In video 116 you saw what
a fork is on the platform.

16
00:01:11,509 --> 00:01:15,091
In the last four videos you
learned what a pull request is.

17
00:01:15,131 --> 00:01:19,205
This video puts the pieces in order,
as one cycle with eight steps.

18
00:01:19,545 --> 00:01:23,643
Then two short sections that
close the pull request module.

19
00:01:23,683 --> 00:01:29,394
Section 17.17, review practice: this is
practice derived from the mechanics of the

20
00:01:29,434 --> 00:01:33,602
chapter, not GitHub documentation,
and I present it as such.

21
00:01:33,642 --> 00:01:37,880
And section 17.18, the display
limits of large pull requests.

22
00:01:38,220 --> 00:01:44,403
One replay, labs/ch17/fork-workflow.sh,
in which bare repositories on disk play

23
00:01:44,443 --> 00:01:51,019
upstream and your fork. Then the complete
cycle of Lab 21.1 as a walkthrough, with a

24
00:01:51,059 --> 00:01:53,303
second account or a teammate.

25
00:01:53,643 --> 00:02:00,254
Labels: git merge --ff-only is 🟡 CAUTION
as a branch-moving command, and git switch

26
00:02:00,294 --> 00:02:06,504
-c is 🟢 SAFE: it adds a ref. git push
origin --delete removes a remote branch.

27
00:02:06,544 --> 00:02:12,753
git branch -D and gh repo sync --force
are 🔴 DANGEROUS in the chapter's table.

28
00:02:12,793 --> 00:02:19,243
I give the five answers for gh repo sync
--force in the concept section, and I do

29
00:02:19,283 --> 00:02:20,125
not run it.

30
00:02:21,965 --> 00:02:23,377
After this video you can:

31
00:02:23,717 --> 00:02:27,374
Name the three repositories of the
fork workflow and the remotes in the

32
00:02:27,414 --> 00:02:28,415
contributor's clone.

33
00:02:28,755 --> 00:02:32,899
Take a change from upstream to fork
to branch to pull request and back.

34
00:02:33,239 --> 00:02:37,715
Fetch and check out a contributor's pull
request as a maintainer, and push to it

35
00:02:37,755 --> 00:02:38,449
when allowed.

36
00:02:38,789 --> 00:02:42,001
Keep the fork's default branch
in sync without merge commits.

37
00:02:42,341 --> 00:02:46,956
Apply the review practices of
section 17.17 to a pull request.

38
00:02:48,796 --> 00:02:53,634
In one sentence: in the fork-and-pull model
you push to a repository you own and ask

39
00:02:53,674 --> 00:02:58,220
the upstream repository to take the
commits. The pull request, your proposal to

40
00:02:58,260 --> 00:03:00,543
merge, lives in the upstream repository.

41
00:03:00,883 --> 00:03:04,318
Three repositories. Upstream,
which a maintainer controls.

42
00:03:04,358 --> 00:03:09,352
Your fork, on the platform, which you control.
And your clone, on your machine, with

43
00:03:09,392 --> 00:03:14,325
two remotes, two names for other repositories:
origin is your fork, upstream is the

44
00:03:14,365 --> 00:03:15,404
shared repository.

45
00:03:15,744 --> 00:03:20,572
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper: draw
the three boxes, and write origin and

46
00:03:20,612 --> 00:03:23,980
upstream on the arrows that
leave your clone. I'll wait.

47
00:03:28,820 --> 00:03:33,195
Two arrows: origin goes to your fork,
upstream to the shared repository.

48
00:03:33,535 --> 00:03:36,881
Platform facts first, from
GitHub's reference pages.

49
00:03:36,921 --> 00:03:41,960
"You do not need permission from the
upstream repository to push to a fork you

50
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:47,234
created." A fork and its upstream share
the same Git data, which you saw in video

51
00:03:47,274 --> 00:03:51,661
116. The author can let maintainers
push to the pull request branch.

52
00:03:52,001 --> 00:03:56,741
That works only for forks owned by a user:
"You cannot give push permissions to a

53
00:03:56,781 --> 00:04:01,698
fork owned by an organization." And for
workflows triggered by a pull request from a

54
00:04:01,738 --> 00:04:06,182
fork, "secrets are not passed to the
runner" except GITHUB_TOKEN, which "has

55
00:04:06,222 --> 00:04:09,959
read-only permissions". The Actions
security videos explain why.

56
00:04:10,299 --> 00:04:14,808
The section ends with three rules, and
the whole workflow follows from them.

57
00:04:14,848 --> 00:04:19,777
Never commit on the fork's default branch.
Branch from upstream/main after a fetch.

58
00:04:19,817 --> 00:04:22,950
One branch per pull request,
deleted after the merge.

59
00:04:23,290 --> 00:04:26,984
Why the first rule? Look at the
three names in today's clone.

60
00:04:27,024 --> 00:04:30,719
The fork's main has one job: to
be a copy of upstream's main.

61
00:04:30,759 --> 00:04:35,372
As long as it has no commits of its own,
syncing it is a fast-forward, and a

62
00:04:35,412 --> 00:04:40,332
fast-forward cannot lose anything. The
moment you commit there, a fast-forward is

63
00:04:40,372 --> 00:04:45,291
impossible, and every way of syncing either
creates a merge commit on your fork's

64
00:04:45,331 --> 00:04:50,189
main, which then shows up in every later
pull request, or discards your commits.

65
00:04:50,529 --> 00:04:56,359
Quick quiz. In the picture, the fork's main
is one commit behind and has nothing of

66
00:04:56,399 --> 00:05:00,672
its own. Syncing it is: A, a
fast-forward. B, a merge commit.

67
00:05:00,712 --> 00:05:02,865
C, a forced reset. Your answer?

68
00:05:07,705 --> 00:05:11,473
A. The label slides forward along
the line, and nothing can be lost.

69
00:05:11,813 --> 00:05:17,686
That is where the 🔴 command comes in. gh
repo sync fast-forwards the fork's branch,

70
00:05:17,726 --> 00:05:21,463
and with --force it hard-resets
it. The five answers.

71
00:05:21,503 --> 00:05:27,305
What it changes: a branch of the fork. What
it can destroy: commits on that branch

72
00:05:27,345 --> 00:05:32,863
that upstream does not have. How to
preview: git rev-list --left-right --count

73
00:05:32,903 --> 00:05:37,636
upstream/main...origin/main, which
counts what each side has alone.

74
00:05:37,976 --> 00:05:42,499
How to recover: push the old commits
back from a clone that still has them.

75
00:05:42,539 --> 00:05:47,487
When it is appropriate: when the fork's
branch is meant to be an exact copy of its

76
00:05:47,527 --> 00:05:51,624
parent, which means after you have
moved any stray work to a branch.

77
00:05:51,964 --> 00:05:56,269
Review practice. In one sentence: a review
is a claim about a specific diff at a

78
00:05:56,309 --> 00:06:00,832
specific commit, so a good reviewer controls
which diff and which commit they looked

79
00:06:00,872 --> 00:06:00,994
at.

80
00:06:01,334 --> 00:06:06,889
As an author. Keep the pull request small.
Check git log --oneline origin/main..HEAD

81
00:06:06,929 --> 00:06:12,217
before you open it. Say what you tested:
for an ML change, which evaluation set,

82
00:06:12,257 --> 00:06:17,212
which metric, which commit. Once review
has started, add commits instead of

83
00:06:17,252 --> 00:06:22,607
rewriting. If you must rewrite, say so,
and keep the content change separate from

84
00:06:22,647 --> 00:06:23,340
the rebase.

85
00:06:23,680 --> 00:06:28,959
As a reviewer. For anything risky, run
the code: gh pr checkout, then the tests.

86
00:06:28,999 --> 00:06:33,680
After a force push, compare the old
and new series with git range-diff.

87
00:06:33,720 --> 00:06:39,132
Approve the commit you read. Give changes
under .github/workflows/, to CODEOWNERS,

88
00:06:39,172 --> 00:06:42,656
to lock files and to deployment
code a second reader.

89
00:06:42,696 --> 00:06:48,041
And treat an automated approval as a signal
about the diff, not about the intent.

90
00:06:48,381 --> 00:06:53,019
What a review cannot see: semantic conflicts
with other open pull requests, files

91
00:06:53,059 --> 00:06:56,888
hidden by diff limits, and anything
the test merge did not include.

92
00:06:56,928 --> 00:06:59,198
Checks on the merged result catch those.

93
00:06:59,538 --> 00:07:04,156
Display limits. In one sentence:
GitHub truncates large pull requests.

94
00:07:04,196 --> 00:07:06,951
The Git data is complete, the page is not.

95
00:07:07,291 --> 00:07:13,280
The total diff of a pull request: 20,000
lines that you can load, or 1 megabyte of

96
00:07:13,320 --> 00:07:19,088
raw diff. One file's diff: 20,000 loadable
lines or 500 kilobytes, of which 400

97
00:07:19,128 --> 00:07:23,940
lines and 20 kilobytes load
automatically. Files in one diff: 300.

98
00:07:23,980 --> 00:07:30,043
Commits listed on compare and pull request
pages: 250, with a note that more exist.

99
00:07:30,383 --> 00:07:36,259
"Rebase and merge": 100 commits. Beyond
a limit, review locally: git diff --stat

100
00:07:36,299 --> 00:07:42,398
base...head has no ceiling. A pull request
that hits these limits is usually one of

101
00:07:42,438 --> 00:07:48,092
the cases from video 125, or a generated
file that should not be in the diff.

102
00:07:48,432 --> 00:07:53,319
A version note: a redesigned "Files
changed" page is the default since the

103
00:07:53,359 --> 00:07:57,181
twenty-second of January 2026.
Distrust older screenshots.

104
00:07:57,221 --> 00:08:02,707
Whether the classic opt-out still exists
is not confirmed in the course's research.

105
00:08:04,547 --> 00:08:09,442
Think of the fork as your outbox on the
platform. You do not write letters in the

106
00:08:09,482 --> 00:08:14,317
outbox. You write them at your desk, the
clone, each on its own sheet, a branch.

107
00:08:14,357 --> 00:08:18,400
You put one sheet into the outbox
and ask the recipient to take it.

108
00:08:18,440 --> 00:08:23,396
The outbox also holds a copy of the
recipient's current rulebook, the fork's main,

109
00:08:23,436 --> 00:08:28,088
and you never write in that copy. You
only replace it with the newer edition.

110
00:08:28,428 --> 00:08:33,164
The model breaks at one point, and you
know it from video 116: a real outbox is

111
00:08:33,204 --> 00:08:36,972
yours alone. A fork shares its
Git data with the whole network.

112
00:08:37,012 --> 00:08:41,203
What you put there is published, and
stays reachable through upstream.

113
00:08:43,043 --> 00:08:48,697
One: fork. Two: clone the fork. Three: fetch
upstream, because the fork may already

114
00:08:48,737 --> 00:08:52,401
be behind. Four: push your
branch to origin, the fork.

115
00:08:52,441 --> 00:08:57,751
Five: the pull request. Look at where
the arrow lands: in the upstream box, as

116
00:08:57,791 --> 00:09:03,514
refs/pull/7/head. The pull request lives
upstream, and so does a ref to your commit.

117
00:09:03,554 --> 00:09:08,110
Six: review round trips, in your
clone. Seven: a maintainer merges.

118
00:09:08,150 --> 00:09:12,843
Eight: fetch upstream, fast-forward
your main, push main to the fork.

119
00:09:13,183 --> 00:09:15,396
Step eight is the one beginners skip.

120
00:09:21,736 --> 00:09:26,603
The count prints two numbers: commits only
upstream has, and commits only your fork

121
00:09:26,643 --> 00:09:31,214
has. For a healthy fork that is merely
behind, what must the second number be?

122
00:09:31,554 --> 00:09:36,951
Zero. Here: one and zero. Upstream has one
commit your fork lacks, and the fork has

123
00:09:36,991 --> 00:09:38,196
nothing of its own.

124
00:09:38,536 --> 00:09:42,541
Start the branch from upstream/main,
not from the fork's stale main.

125
00:09:42,881 --> 00:09:47,806
--no-track keeps the branch from
adopting upstream/main as its upstream.

126
00:09:47,846 --> 00:09:52,289
git push -u origin sets the fork's
branch as the upstream branch.

127
00:09:52,329 --> 00:09:54,909
You could not push to upstream anyway.

128
00:09:55,249 --> 00:09:59,367
Opening the pull request puts the head
commit into the upstream repository.

129
00:09:59,407 --> 00:10:01,030
Imitated on the bare upstream:

130
00:10:01,370 --> 00:10:06,293
The commit is now stored in upstream, under
refs/pull/7/head, on none of upstream's

131
00:10:06,333 --> 00:10:06,831
branches.

132
00:10:07,171 --> 00:10:11,546
Now the maintainer's side. Asha takes
the pull request into her clone.

133
00:10:11,886 --> 00:10:14,981
That is what gh pr
checkout 7 does for her.

134
00:10:17,321 --> 00:10:22,543
She adds a test. Where can she push it so
that it becomes part of your pull request?

135
00:10:22,883 --> 00:10:27,070
To your branch in your fork, which the
maintainer-edit permission allows.

136
00:10:27,110 --> 00:10:29,792
Not to refs/pull/7/head:
that ref is read-only.

137
00:10:30,132 --> 00:10:34,615
Your branch moved without you. The log
shows two authors on your pull request.

138
00:10:34,955 --> 00:10:36,705
So pull before you add anything.

139
00:10:43,045 --> 00:10:47,873
Asha merges into upstream's main. Your
main and your fork's main have not heard

140
00:10:47,913 --> 00:10:48,674
about it yet.

141
00:10:49,014 --> 00:10:53,781
Now step eight. Predict the two numbers
the count will print when we're done.

142
00:10:53,821 --> 00:10:54,905
Say them out loud.

143
00:10:59,745 --> 00:11:05,189
--ff-only is the safety: if your main had
commits of its own, this command would

144
00:11:05,229 --> 00:11:08,822
refuse, and you would know
at once. It fast-forwards.

145
00:11:09,162 --> 00:11:14,485
Push main to the fork, and the count is
zero and zero: fork and upstream agree.

146
00:11:14,525 --> 00:11:20,119
On GitHub the same sync is the fork's sync
control or gh repo sync without --force.

147
00:11:26,259 --> 00:11:30,809
One branch per pull request, deleted
after the merge, locally and in the fork.

148
00:11:31,149 --> 00:11:34,593
Fork a practice repository
that the second account owns.

149
00:11:34,633 --> 00:11:38,201
You saw where the fork relation
is displayed in video 116.

150
00:11:38,241 --> 00:11:42,556
Clone the fork and add the second
remote. Then follow the eight steps.

151
00:11:42,596 --> 00:11:47,409
Open the pull request from your fork's
branch against the upstream repository.

152
00:11:47,449 --> 00:11:52,636
On the form, the documentation describes a
setting that allows edits by maintainers.

153
00:11:52,676 --> 00:11:54,502
From the maintainer's account:

154
00:11:54,842 --> 00:11:59,934
Review, merge. Back in your clone, do step
eight by hand, with the commands from the

155
00:11:59,974 --> 00:12:03,050
replay, before you try the
platform's sync control:

156
00:12:03,390 --> 00:12:07,230
At each step, say which ref moved
in which of the three repositories.

157
00:12:07,270 --> 00:12:09,592
That sentence is the objective of the lab.

158
00:12:11,432 --> 00:12:13,080
Five mistakes to watch for.

159
00:12:13,420 --> 00:12:18,406
Committing on the fork's default branch.
Root cause: that branch can then no longer

160
00:12:18,446 --> 00:12:23,372
be fast-forwarded to upstream, and every
later pull request from a branch based on

161
00:12:23,412 --> 00:12:25,128
it carries the stray commits.

162
00:12:25,468 --> 00:12:28,925
Branching from the fork's main
without fetching upstream.

163
00:12:28,965 --> 00:12:33,895
Root cause: the fork's main is as old as
the last sync; the pull request range is

164
00:12:33,935 --> 00:12:35,306
taken against upstream.

165
00:12:35,646 --> 00:12:39,672
Running gh repo sync --force
to fix a fork that is ahead.

166
00:12:39,712 --> 00:12:45,593
Root cause: with --force the sync is a hard
reset and discards the commits upstream

167
00:12:45,633 --> 00:12:46,592
does not have.

168
00:12:46,932 --> 00:12:51,251
Pushing to a pull request branch after a
maintainer edited it, without pulling.

169
00:12:51,291 --> 00:12:55,390
Root cause: the branch in your fork
moved; your push is a non-fast-forward.

170
00:12:55,730 --> 00:12:59,458
Approving a large pull request
from the web page alone.

171
00:12:59,498 --> 00:13:05,076
Root cause: the page truncates beyond its
display limits; the Git data is complete

172
00:13:05,116 --> 00:13:08,297
and git diff --stat
base...head has no ceiling.

173
00:13:10,137 --> 00:13:15,036
Now, out of the lab, and back to the hook.
The contributor, repaired without losing

174
00:13:15,076 --> 00:13:19,737
anything. The count says three and
forty-one: her fork's main has three commits

175
00:13:19,777 --> 00:13:24,735
upstream lacks. First she keeps them: a new
branch at the current main, so the three

176
00:13:24,775 --> 00:13:29,496
commits have a name. Then her local main
is set back to match upstream/main, and

177
00:13:29,536 --> 00:13:30,627
pushed to the fork.

178
00:13:30,967 --> 00:13:35,778
Because the fork's main is being moved
backwards, that push is forced, on a branch

179
00:13:35,818 --> 00:13:39,447
only she uses, after the stray
work is safe on its own branch.

180
00:13:39,487 --> 00:13:44,299
Now the count is zero and zero. The three
commits live on a branch, from which she

181
00:13:44,339 --> 00:13:47,908
can open a proper pull request
or cherry-pick what she wants.

182
00:13:48,248 --> 00:13:52,111
Lab 21.1 has you make exactly
this mistake and recover.

183
00:13:52,151 --> 00:13:58,001
Had she run gh repo sync --force first, the
three commits would have been gone from

184
00:13:58,041 --> 00:14:02,259
the fork, recoverable only from
a clone that still had them.

185
00:14:02,599 --> 00:14:06,417
The maintainer's side of the same
project follows section 17.17.

186
00:14:06,457 --> 00:14:11,481
For a change to the scoring code, check out
the pull request and run the evaluation.

187
00:14:11,521 --> 00:14:16,063
After the contributor's force push,
compare old and new with git range-diff.

188
00:14:16,103 --> 00:14:21,007
And ask the author which evaluation set,
which metric and which commit the numbers

189
00:14:21,047 --> 00:14:22,695
in the description refer to.

190
00:14:24,535 --> 00:14:29,065
Your turn. Do Lab 21.1, "A full
fork and pull request cycle", in

191
00:14:29,105 --> 00:14:33,207
lab-manual/m21-pull-requests-forks.md,
the complete cycle.

192
00:14:33,247 --> 00:14:37,562
The local part runs in the lab
shell with three repositories.

193
00:14:37,602 --> 00:14:41,918
Then repeat it on GitHub with a
second account or a teammate.

194
00:14:41,958 --> 00:14:47,488
At every step, before you run it, say
which ref will move in which repository.

195
00:14:47,528 --> 00:14:53,201
Before the failure scenario, predict what
git merge --ff-only upstream/main will

196
00:14:53,241 --> 00:14:53,486
say.

197
00:14:53,826 --> 00:15:00,074
The challenge is Exercise 21.5, "The
fork that cannot be synced", in

198
00:15:00,114 --> 00:15:02,663
exercises/m19-m25-github.md.

199
00:15:04,503 --> 00:15:07,817
Question 266 of the CTO question bank:

200
00:15:10,157 --> 00:15:15,025
Answer out loud. A strong answer states the
condition under which a fast-forward is

201
00:15:15,065 --> 00:15:19,816
impossible and how to measure it. It gives
the order of the repair, with the step

202
00:15:19,856 --> 00:15:23,305
that makes the stray commits
safe before anything is reset.

203
00:15:23,345 --> 00:15:28,154
It says exactly what the forced sync does
to the branch, and it ends with the rule

204
00:15:28,194 --> 00:15:29,810
that prevents the situation.

205
00:15:31,650 --> 00:15:35,971
Let's land this. Three names for main,
all on one commit: that's a fork in good

206
00:15:36,011 --> 00:15:38,289
health, and you know how to keep it there.

207
00:15:38,629 --> 00:15:40,289
You should now be able to say:

208
00:15:40,629 --> 00:15:45,162
Three repositories: upstream, your fork,
your clone, with origin for the fork and

209
00:15:45,202 --> 00:15:47,138
upstream for the shared repository.

